{
"And I have never failed to fault the men of that time: their foresight of danger was thin, their sight of advantage shallow, and they did not understand the shape of the empire.": "86e8e87dd297",
"And so they let the men of Ch'in watch for the gap and take their states. Is that not to be grieved over?": "0972208a807b",
"And that Ch'in — what could it have done?": "8cba2da9d830",
"Before Ch'in's troops had even set out, the lords of the empire had already worn themselves down.": "7a4ce58eb360",
"For Ch'in to have Han and Wei beside it was like a man having a disease in his vitals.": "9c6a7e9ba590",
"For Ch'in to use troops against Yen and Chao was a dangerous business for Ch'in.": "ce431ed9edbc",
"From this, what Ch'in feared may be seen.": "a49a52392cf8",
"Han and Wei blocked Ch'in's highway and screened the lords east of the mountains; so nothing in the empire mattered as much as Han and Wei.": "da2dc8437e0d",
"Han and Wei could not stand against Ch'in alone, yet the lords of the empire relied on them to screen their western side. Nothing therefore would have been better than to treat Han generously and keep Wei close, and so shut Ch'in out.": "66e45cd856b6",
"Han and Wei were the lords' own barrier, and they let the men of Ch'in come and go through it. Is that understanding the shape of the empire?": "72c2e8e64424",
"I have often thought long and deeply about it, believing there must have been some plan by which they could have made themselves safe.": "e12720b5c39b",
"I have read the Hereditary Houses of the Six States, and privately wondered at it: the lords of the empire, with five times the land and ten times the people, roused themselves and faced west to attack a Ch'in holding a thousand li beyond the mountains — and yet could not escape destruction.": "f8dc8f8f827a",
"Let two states face Ch'in while four rested within and quietly helped them at need: in this way they could have met a war without end.": "b7c9a4aee887",
"Long ago, when Fan Chü was employed in Ch'in he took in Han; when Shang Yang was employed in Ch'in he took in Wei.": "3cf9869c4ca6",
"Once Han and Wei broke and went over, the men of Ch'in could pass their troops through to the eastern lords, and the whole empire suffered the disaster.": "5909ddbda07a",
"Then the men of Ch'in would not have dared pass Han and Wei to spy on Ch'i, Ch'u, Yen and Chao, and those four states could have kept themselves whole behind them.": "2111f15f1bfb",
"They abandoned the small states of Han and Wei to stand alone against a Ch'in strong as tiger and wolf. How could those two not break and go over to Ch'in?": "cc6dd90cbff8",
"They did not see this. Instead they coveted a foot or an inch of border land, broke their oaths and ruined their covenants, and slaughtered one another.": "b3af849ab2c4",
"To go past Han and cross Wei in order to attack another state's capital, with Yen and Chao resisting in front and Han and Wei striking from behind — that is a dangerous road.": "d903cc9cb505",
"What Ch'in contended for with the lords was not in Ch'i, Ch'u, Yen or Chao, but in the borderlands of Han and Wei;": "6f5fc1093c39",
"When King Chao, not yet having won the hearts of Han and Wei, sent troops to attack Kang and Shou in Ch'i, Fan Chü was troubled by it.": "24df79811954",
"With four states at peace supporting Han and Wei who faced the enemy, Han and Wei would have had no anxiety behind them, and could have gone out on the empire's behalf to meet Ch'in's troops.": "1a20d05471fd",
"Yet when Ch'in attacked Yen and Chao it never had to fear Han and Wei — because Han and Wei had attached themselves to Ch'in.": "7658bbba325e",
"and what the lords contended for with Ch'in was not in Ch'i, Ch'u, Yen or Chao, but in the fields of Han and Wei.": "65cc7fcb3ad9"
}
